<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ben-Hadad III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 800 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> · Also known as Bar-Hadad III</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ben-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Hadad</a> III, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> Bar-Hadad, was king of Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> around 800 BCE, son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazael">Hazael</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakkur">Zakkur</a> stele records that he led a coalition of kings that besieged Zakkur, king of Hamath and Luash, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-afis">Hazrek</a>. His reign saw the decline of Damascus, which suffered defeats by Israel and the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adad-nirari-iii">Adad-nirari III</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ben-hadad-iii.jpg" alt="Ben-Hadad III" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rama · CC BY-SA 2.0 fr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zakkur_Stele_0154.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
